A||||6||Spektrum-87GT||want to rebuild a 350 sbc, where do i start?||||1||
Z||000000||Spektrum-87GT||10-09-2001||09:00 PM||||ok, my mechanical skills are decent for a do-it-yourself'er, always finish what i start. I know a quite a bit about engines from reading auto mechanics books, but its time to apply these things to the real world. I'm going to a junk yard tomorrow, getting a 350 small block chevy. My question is, if i wanted to build up a ~300hp motor, what would be the best way to go? as in... short block out of a late 80's early 90's camaro/firebird? or should i try to get a long block? or should i just start from scratch with the block? I'm definitly going to do a rebuild with high performance parts, but not building a stroker. Any tips on the subject would be appreciated because as you can see, i'm pretty clueless  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> also, how much should I expect to spend?(not the entire rebuild, just for a short or long block)||||reg||1
Z||000001||samoht||10-09-2001||09:06 PM||||i would start with an 87 and newer 5.7L and get it complete.. if you are just shooting for 300hp, I would stick with a nice Iroc motor, at lest mutiport injection.. and desent machine shop with the right pistons should be able to get you around 300hp without going roller.. head are key.. but if you can find a desent part of late model head.. 300 hp should be ok with a good cleaning and valve job<P>85GT3.2||||reg||1
Z||000002||California Kid||10-09-2001||09:09 PM||||At todays prices for crate motors, you would have to be nuts to build up a junk yard motor. It's better to wait a little longer, save up the money, no guess work doing it this way.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||10-09-2001||10:09 PM||||Hey Spektrum!<BR>I am in the process of building up 2 SBC's at the moment...<BR>One is for a show car and the other is for a go car...<BR>Both blocks are 4 bolt main cap, 350 cubic inch engines...<BR>MOST truck and van engines are 4 bolt main cap, and are usually not any more expensive than the 2 bolt main...<BR>If you are looking for 300 HP, I would stick to the flat top "eyebrow" pistons at 9:1 compression ratio, 1.94 inch small chamber heads, a Victor JR. intake, 650 Holley vacuume secondary carb, I have a favorite cam, but can't find the specs!! Something around .45 or .47 lift..., and a set of headers with small diameter/long length primary tubes and 3 inch or 4 inch diameter collectors...<BR>HOWEVER, if you want 400+ HP, get the Holley System Max II...<BR>It is a kit with most everything matched to get gobs of power!!!<BR>Have fun and learn the best engine ever made!<BR><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">        <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||KissMySSFiero||10-09-2001||11:26 PM||||i agree with CaL Kid,<BR>for $1300 bucks you can get a brand new 4bolt 350 right from your gm dealer.  the motor is the LM1.  archie uses it a lot in his in house swaps(his Finale)   I believe its rated at 250hp.  with a few bolts ons like headers or a good intake you can get that up.  or you can match it with a good cam and i see no reason you cant get 300hp with that. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||LottaBallsCamaro||10-09-2001||11:58 PM||||I would reccomend a 87 or newer block. It Wont matter if it is a 2 or 4 bolt unless you want to spin it any faster then 6,000RPM.<BR>Find a rebuilt set of 91 or 92 Vette heads and have them ported. Then if you go with TPI, put an LT1 intake on it (a bit of a higher end intake). There are instructions on thirdgen.org on how to do this. With a good cam you should get there. If you go carbed and are looking for top end power then go with a vitor Jr. but otherwise go with an RPM airgap.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jimmy||10-10-2001||12:49 AM||||You can use some vortec heads.  Fully assembled run you just over $400, add some of the General's 1.6 rockers for $60.  They are the best stock iron heads Chevy has ever made.<P>  A stock 350 bottom end with flatop pistons, Vortec heads w/1.6 rockers, Edelbrock RPM Airgap intake or Victor JR, Extreme Energy cam 268, 650-750 holley, and headers will put you over 400 HP and well over 400 FTlbs of torque.  This engine would have a 10:1 compresion that would be 91 92 octane friendly.<P>Do a search on the forums over at <A HREF="http://www.chevytalk.com" TARGET=_blank>www.chevytalk.com</A>  for "vortec".  You will find lots of winning combos with these awesome cheap heads.  Pick up any muscle magazine and you'll find at least one article about these heads.<P>One thing you should know, you need to buy a vortec style intake as it bolts differently than other small block intakes.<P>Bolt these heads onto an already great ZZ4 crate motor and you would gain 15 HP and 10 ftlbs on average.<P>Hope this helps!<P>Jim <BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT<BR>MSCF<p>[This message has been edited by Jimmy (edited 10-10-2001).]||||reg||1
